e book, and verify it.\n\nWritten response: {{response}}" 13194139539421 17592186576411 false] [17592186484433 1071 "**(a)** Prove or otherwise convince yourself that $\\mathbf F$ is continuous/differentiable at $(x,y)$ if and only if $\\mathbf G$ is continuous/differentiable there." 13194139539421 17592186576411 true] [17592186484433 1071 "**(a)** Prove or otherwise convince yourself that $\\mathbf F$ is continuous/differentiable at $(x,y)$ if and only if $\\mathbf G$ is continuous/differentiable there.\n\nWritten response: {{response}}" 13194139539421 17592186576411 false] [17592186484447 1071 "**(c)** Is $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ in reduced row-echelon form? Explain." 13194139539421 17592186576411 true] [17592186484447 1071 "**(c)** Is $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ in reduced row-echelon form? Explain.\n\nWritten response: {{response}}" 13194139539421 17592186576411 false] [17592186484461 1071 "**(e)** $A$ is $4\\times2$, $\\operatorname{rank}A=2$, $\\operatorname{rank}[A\\mid B]=2$. State whether these data are possible for $AX=B$. If possible, describe the solution set. Explain." 13194139539421 17592186576411 true] [17592186484461 1071 "**(e)** $A$ is $4\\times2$, $\\operatorname{rank}A=2$, $\\operatorname{rank}[A\\mid B]=2$. State whether these data are possible for $AX=B$. If possible, describe the solution set. Explain.\n\nWritten response: {{response}}" 13194139539421 17592186576411 false] [17592186484462 1071 "**(g)** Find $BE$ if possible; otherwise explain why it is not defined." 13194139539421 17592186576411 true] [17592186484462 1071 "**(g)** Find $BE$ if possible; otherwise explain why it is not defined.\n\nWritten response: {{response}}" 13194139539421 17592186576411 false] [17592186484467 1071 "Source question: Exercise **1.2.4**, [printed page 42 (PDF page 54)](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf#page=54).\n\nDo the planes $x+y-3z=2$, $2x+y+z=1$, and $3x+2y-2z=0$ have a common point? If so, find one; otherwise explain why none exists." 13194139539421 17592186576411 true] [17592186484467 1071 "Source question: Exercise **1.2.4**, [printed page 42 (PDF page 54)](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf#page=54).\n\nDo the planes $x+y-3z=2$, $2x+y+z=1$, and $3x+2y-2z=0$ have a common point? If so, find one; otherwise explain why none exists.\n\nWritten response: {{response}}" 13194139539421 17592186576411 false] [17592186484469 1071 "**(a)** $A$ is $5\\times6$, $\\operatorname{rank}A=4$, $\\operatorname{rank}[A\\mid B]=4$. State whether these data are possible for $AX=B$. If possible, describe the solution set. Explain." 13194139539421 17592186576411 true] [17592186484469 1071 "**(a)** $A$ is $5\\times6$, $\\operatorname{rank}A=4$, $\\operatorname{rank}[A\\mid B]=4$. State whether these data are possible for $AX=B$. If possible, describe the solution set. Explain.\n\nWritten response: {{response}}" 13194139539421 17592186576411 false] [17592186484473 1071 "Source question: Exercise **2.1.50**, [printed page 97 (PDF page 109)](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf#page=109).\n\nFor invertible square matrices A and B of the same size, show $(AB)^{-1}=B^{-1}A^{-1}$ by verifying $(AB)(B^{-1}A^{-1})=I$ and $(B^{-1}A^{-1})(AB)=I$. Hint: use Exercise 2.1.48 (Problem 18)." 13194139539421 17592186576411 true] [17592186484473 1071 "Source question: Exercise **2.1.50**, [printed page 97 (PDF page 109)](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf#page=109).\n\nFor invertible square matrices A and B of the same size, show